---
const cues = [
  {
    label: '01 / COLOR',
    title: 'What tones dominate?',
    image: 'satellite',
    alt: 'Original Hurricane Matthew satellite scene from the EDA notebook, with vegetation, pale linear boundaries and scattered structures.',
    description:
      'RGB statistics describe the palette. Water, vegetation and exposed ground can produce different color distributions.',
    method: 'Channel means or quartiles',
  },
  {
    label: '02 / EDGES',
    title: 'Where does contrast change?',
    image: 'edges',
    alt: 'Saved Sobel response of the same scene: strong intensity changes appear as bright lines against a dark background.',
    description:
      'An edge is a sharp change in brightness: for example, a roof against the ground or a riverbank. Sobel makes these transitions visible.',
    method: 'Mean Sobel magnitude in each block',
  },
  {
    label: '03 / TEXTURE',
    title: 'How do nearby pixels vary?',
    image: 'texture',
    alt: 'Saved local binary pattern representation of the same satellite scene, showing local brightness patterns.',
    description:
      'Texture is the local pattern of light and dark: smooth water, repeated roof tiles or irregular vegetation. LBP compares each pixel with its neighbours.',
    method: 'Quartiles of local binary pattern codes',
  },
];
---

<div class="visual-overview">
  <section class="vision-task" aria-labelledby="reading-disaster-images">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Satellite imagery · xView2 subset</p>
      <h2 id="reading-disaster-images">
        What can an image
        <br />
        tell us after a <em>disaster?</em>
      </h2>
      <p>
        The goal was to turn satellite images into useful information for
        disaster assessment. We tested whether{' '}
        <strong>explicit visual features</strong>
        and lightweight classifiers could identify an event and distinguish
        damage levels.
      </p>
      <p class="dataset-note">600 images · 200 per event · RGB imagery</p>
    </div>
    <div class="task-pair">
      <div class="task-card">
        <span class="eyebrow">Task A / 400 images</span>
        <h3>Which disaster?</h3>
        <div class="event-pair">
          <span>Midwest flooding</span>
          <b aria-hidden="true">↔</b>
          <span>SoCal fire</span>
        </div>
        <p>Binary classification from scene appearance.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="task-card">
        <span class="eyebrow">Task B / 200 images</span>
        <h3>How much damage?</h3>
        <div
          class="damage-scale"
          aria-label="Four damage classes: 0 no damage, 1 minor, 2 major, 3 destroyed"
        >
          <span>
            <b>0</b>No damage
          </span>
          <span>
            <b>1</b>Minor
          </span>
          <span>
            <b>2</b>Major
          </span>
          <span>
            <b>3</b>Destroyed
          </span>
        </div>
        <p>Four damage categories within Hurricane Matthew.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="image-cues" aria-labelledby="image-features">
    <header class="section-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">From pixels to measurements</p>
        <h2 id="image-features">
          One image.
          <br />
          Three ways to read it.
        </h2>
      </div>
      <p>
        Color, edges and texture describe different aspects of an image. These
        are <strong>input features</strong>: a bright contour or a textured
        region is not, by itself, evidence of damage.
      </p>
    </header>
    <div class="cue-grid">
      {cues.map((cue) => (
        <figure class={`cue cue-${cue.image}`}>
          <p class="eyebrow">{cue.label}</p>
          <img
            src={`/media/visual-learning/${cue.image}.png`}
            width="370"
            height="370"
            alt={cue.alt}
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption>
            <h3>{cue.title}</h3>
            <p>{cue.description}</p>
            <span class="method-tag">{cue.method}</span>
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      ))}
    </div>
    <p class="source-note">
      Same Hurricane Matthew scene and its saved transformations from the EDA
      notebook. These are feature maps, not predicted damage masks.
    </p>
    <div class="pixel-examples">
      <div>
        <div
          class="edge-example"
          aria-label="Illustration: dark pixels next to light pixels create a boundary."
        >
          <span></span>
          <span></span>
          <span></span>
          <i></i>
          <span></span>
          <span></span>
          <span></span>
        </div>
        <p>
          <strong>Edge = a boundary.</strong> A dark-to-light transition gives a
          strong gradient.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div
          class="texture-example"
          aria-label="Illustration: a smooth patch compared with a repeated light and dark pattern."
        >
          <span></span>
          <b aria-hidden="true">↔</b>
          <span></span>
        </div>
        <p>
          <strong>Texture = a neighbourhood pattern.</strong> Similar average
          brightness can hide very different local arrangements.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="vision-pipeline" aria-labelledby="visual-learning-pipeline">
    <header class="section-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">The modeling pipeline</p>
        <h2 id="visual-learning-pipeline">
          Summarize locally.
          <br />
          Learn the decision.
        </h2>
      </div>
      <p>
        A global average can hide a damaged corner. Dividing an image into
        blocks retains a little spatial information while keeping the feature
        vector small. The classifier learns how to combine those measurements.
      </p>
    </header>
    <figure class="pipeline-figure">
      <picture>
        <source
          media="(max-width: 700px)"
          srcset="/media/visual-learning/pipeline-mobile.svg"
        />
        <img
          src="/media/visual-learning/pipeline.svg"
          width="1180"
          height="340"
          loading="lazy"
          alt="Pipeline: RGB image, fixed color/Sobel/LBP feature extraction, compact vector, logistic regression and class probabilities. Training labels and classification loss update the classifier weights; the image filters remain fixed."
        />
      </picture>
    </figure>
    <div class="recipe-grid">
      <div class="recipe">
        <span class="eyebrow">Task A / Flood versus fire</span>
        <h3>24 features → L2 logistic regression</h3>
        <div class="feature-counts">
          <span>
            <b>3</b>RGB means
          </span>
          <span>
            <b>9</b>Sobel · 3 × 3
          </span>
          <span>
            <b>12</b>LBP · 2 × 2
          </span>
        </div>
        <p>
          One edge mean per block; three LBP quartiles per block. Five-fold
          validation selects the regularization setting.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="recipe">
        <span class="eyebrow">Task B / Four damage classes</span>
        <h3>25 features → weighted logistic regression</h3>
        <div class="feature-counts">
          <span>
            <b>9</b>RGB quartiles
          </span>
          <span>
            <b>4</b>Sobel · 2 × 2
          </span>
          <span>
            <b>12</b>LBP · 2 × 2
          </span>
        </div>
        <p>
          <strong>StandardScaler</strong> aligns feature scales. Class weights
          penalize errors on scarce classes; horizontal flips add training
          examples for major damage.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="model-choice">
      For damage, we explored Random Forest and ordinal logistic regression
      before using <strong>weighted multinomial logistic regression</strong>. It
      learns one score per class, converted into probabilities by softmax.
    </p>
    <details class="correlation-evidence">
      <summary>Feature checks: what the correlation matrix reveals</summary>
      <div class="correlation-layout">
        <div>
          <h3>More features do not always mean more information.</h3>
          <p>
            The 25-feature matrix shows strong redundancy among color
            statistics: the lower red and green quartiles correlate at{' '}
            <strong>0.95</strong>. Local edge and texture measurements bring
            different patterns of association.
          </p>
          <p>
            We used distributions and correlations to inspect the
            representation. Correlation alone does not establish predictive
            importance or prove that a feature improves the model.
          </p>
          <p class="source-note">
            Original notebook heatmap, also shown on presentation slide 16.
            Blank cells indicate undefined correlations, not zero correlation.
          </p>
          <a href="/media/visual-learning/correlation.png">
            Open the full matrix ↗
          </a>
        </div>
        <img
          src="/media/visual-learning/correlation.png"
          width="1547"
          height="1373"
          loading="lazy"
          alt="Original correlation matrix of 25 Hurricane Matthew features, with highly correlated RGB quartiles, local edge measurements and LBP statistics. One constant LBP statistic has undefined correlations."
        />
      </div>
    </details>
  </section>

  <section class="vision-results" aria-labelledby="visual-learning-results">
    <header class="section-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Evaluation & findings</p>
        <h2 id="visual-learning-results">
          Recognizing the event
          <br />
          is easier than grading damage.
        </h2>
      </div>
      <p>
        Damage classes overlap visually and are unevenly represented. Hurricane
        Matthew has only <strong>6 major-damage images out of 200</strong>.
        Class-level results matter more than a single aggregate score.
      </p>
    </header>
    <div class="score-grid">
      <div>
        <span class="eyebrow">Task A / Reported five-fold validation</span>
        <strong class="score">
          99.0<span>%</span>
        </strong>
        <p>
          Mean accuracy · flood versus fire
          <br />
          Fold range: 97.5–100%.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="eyebrow">Task B / Saved final notebook run</span>
        <strong class="score">0.403</strong>
        <p>
          Test macro-F1 · 40 held-out images
          <br />
          Weighted F1: 0.57 · accuracy: 50%.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="chart-grid">
      <figure>
        <h3>Choosing regularization</h3>
        <img
          src="/media/visual-learning/damage-validation.svg"
          width="620"
          height="380"
          loading="lazy"
          alt="Damage classification: training macro-F1 rises from 0.520 to 0.751 as C increases. Validation macro-F1 is 0.420, 0.533, 0.480, 0.518 and 0.524; C equals 0.1 is selected."
        />
        <figcaption>
          Saved five-fold scores. Lower C means stronger regularization. C = 0.1
          gives the highest validation macro-F1, 0.533.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <h3>Reading class-level performance</h3>
        <img
          src="/media/visual-learning/damage-class-f1.svg"
          width="620"
          height="380"
          loading="lazy"
          alt="Final test F1 by damage class: no damage 0.43 with 6 examples, minor 0.45 with 14, major 0 with 1, destroyed 0.73 with 19."
        />
        <figcaption>
          F1 balances precision and recall. Macro-F1 weights every class
          equally; weighted F1 weights classes by their sample counts.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <div class="result-reading">
      <p>
        <strong>The useful result:</strong> a compact representation separates
        the two event datasets well. It provides a transparent baseline with an
        inspectable path from image to decision.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>The unresolved challenge:</strong> the model misses the only
        major-damage example in the test split. Its zero F1 is a warning, but
        one example is too little to estimate performance reliably.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</div>

<style>
  .visual-overview {
    margin-block: 2.5rem;
    --vision: #3f706e;
    --vision-muted: #63746b;
    --vision-line: #c9d0c5;
    --vision-paper: #f5f3eb;
  }
  h2 {
    font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 3.1vw, 3rem);
    line-height: 1.13;
    margin: 0.65rem 0 1.2rem;
    scroll-margin-top: 2rem;
  }
  h3 {
    font: 600 0.97rem/1.5 var(--font-body);
    margin: 0 0 0.7rem;
  }
  p {
    color: #61695f;
    font-size: 0.86rem;
    line-height: 1.8;
  }
  strong {
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }
  figure {
    margin: 0;
    min-width: 0;
  }
  img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }
  .eyebrow {
    color: var(--vision);
  }
  .vision-task {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1.05fr;
    gap: 3rem;
    align-items: center;
    padding: clamp(1.3rem, 3vw, 2.6rem);
    background: #e5e9df;
    border-top: 1px solid #8da39a;
  }
  .vision-task em {
    color: var(--vision);
    font-weight: 400;
  }
  .dataset-note {
    margin: 1.3rem 0 0;
    font: 0.65rem/1.7 var(--font-label);
  }
  .task-pair {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1rem;
  }
  .task-card {
    padding: 1.2rem 1.4rem;
    background: #f5f3eba6;
    border: 1px solid var(--vision-line);
  }
  .task-card h3 {
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
  }
  .task-card p {
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
  .event-pair {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.8rem;
    font-size: 0.77rem;
  }
  .event-pair span {
    padding: 0.4rem 0.6rem;
    background: #dce5df;
    color: #3d6968;
  }
  .event-pair span:last-child {
    background: #ebded0;
    color: #936041;
  }
  .event-pair b {
    font-weight: 400;
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }
  .damage-scale {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);
    gap: 0.45rem;
  }
  .damage-scale span {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.35rem;
    font: 0.6rem/1.5 var(--font-label);
  }
  .damage-scale b {
    display: block;
    padding: 0.35rem;
    background: #dae4d8;
    color: #55735c;
    font: 1.4rem/1 var(--font-display);
  }
  .damage-scale span:nth-child(2) b {
    background: #e3e2ce;
    color: #7e7f46;
  }
  .damage-scale span:nth-child(3) b {
    background: #ead8bd;
    color: #a37b38;
  }
  .damage-scale span:nth-child(4) b {
    background: #e7cdc0;
    color: #a35c41;
  }
  .image-cues,
  .vision-pipeline,
  .vision-results {
    margin-top: 3rem;
  }
  .section-heading {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    align-items: end;
    gap: 3rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.4rem;
  }
  .section-heading h2 {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
  .section-heading > p {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .cue-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    gap: 1.2rem;
  }
  .cue {
    background: var(--vision-paper);
    padding: 1.1rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--vision-line);
  }
  .cue > .eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 0.8rem;
    font-size: 0.6rem;
  }
  .cue > img {
    aspect-ratio: 1.35;
    object-fit: cover;
  }
  .cue figcaption h3 {
    margin: 1rem 0 0.55rem;
    font-size: 0.87rem;
  }
  .cue figcaption p {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.76rem;
  }
  .method-tag {
    display: block;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--vision-line);
    margin-top: 1rem;
    padding-top: 0.65rem;
    font: 0.59rem/1.6 var(--font-label);
    color: var(--vision);
  }
  .source-note {
    font: 0.6rem/1.8 var(--font-label);
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }
  .pixel-examples {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 2rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--vision-line);
    padding: 1rem 0 1.2rem;
  }
  .pixel-examples > div {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 1rem;
  }
  .pixel-examples p {
    font-size: 0.74rem;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .edge-example {
    display: flex;
    position: relative;
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }
  .edge-example span {
    width: 16px;
    height: 42px;
    background: #54685d;
    border-right: 1px solid #6b7a6e;
  }
  .edge-example span:nth-last-child(-n + 3) {
    background: #b8c9ad;
    border-color: #cad6bf;
  }
  .edge-example i {
    height: 54px;
    width: 3px;
    position: absolute;
    top: -6px;
    left: 46px;
    background: #b67546;
  }
  .texture-example {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }
  .texture-example span {
    width: 45px;
    height: 45px;
    background: #90a08b;
  }
  .texture-example span:last-child {
    background: repeating-conic-gradient(#54685d 0% 25%, #cbd8b9 0% 50%) 50% /
      10px 10px;
  }
  .texture-example b {
    color: #86917e;
    font-weight: 400;
  }
  .pipeline-figure {
    border: 1px solid var(--vision-line);
    background: var(--vision-paper);
    padding: 0.6rem;
  }
  .recipe-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 2rem;
    margin-top: 1.3rem;
  }
  .recipe {
    border-top: 2px solid #69847a;
    padding-top: 1rem;
  }
  .recipe:nth-child(2) {
    border-color: #a17851;
  }
  .recipe h3 {
    margin-top: 0.55rem;
  }
  .recipe p,
  .model-choice {
    font-size: 0.78rem;
  }
  .feature-counts {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    gap: 0.6rem;
    margin-block: 1rem;
  }
  .feature-counts span {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    font: 0.6rem/1.6 var(--font-label);
    color: #607369;
  }
  .feature-counts b {
    font: 1.6rem/1 var(--font-display);
    color: var(--vision);
  }
  .model-choice {
    margin: 0.6rem 0 1.2rem;
  }
  .correlation-evidence {
    border-block: 1px solid var(--vision-line);
    padding: 1rem 1.2rem;
    background: #e5e9df;
  }
  summary {
    font: 500 0.8rem/1.6 var(--font-body);
    color: var(--vision);
    cursor: pointer;
  }
  .correlation-layout {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1.25fr;
    gap: 2rem;
    align-items: center;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }
  .correlation-layout p {
    font-size: 0.77rem;
  }
  .correlation-layout a {
    font-size: 0.72rem;
  }
  .score-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 1.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }
  .score-grid > div {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: auto 1fr;
    align-items: center;
    column-gap: 1.7rem;
    padding: 1.3rem 1.5rem;
    background: #e5e9df;
    border-top: 2px solid #69847a;
  }
  .score-grid > div:nth-child(2) {
    background: #ebe3d7;
    border-color: #a17851;
  }
  .score-grid .eyebrow {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
    margin-bottom: 0.7rem;
  }
  .score {
    font: 3.5rem/1 var(--font-display);
    color: var(--vision);
  }
  .score span {
    font-size: 1.8rem;
  }
  .score-grid p {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.7rem;
  }
  .chart-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 1.5rem;
  }
  .chart-grid figure {
    padding: 1.2rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--vision-line);
    background: var(--vision-paper);
  }
  .chart-grid h3 {
    font-size: 0.86rem;
  }
  .chart-grid figcaption {
    font: 0.6rem/1.8 var(--font-label);
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }
  .result-reading {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 2rem;
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
  }
  .result-reading p {
    font-size: 0.78rem;
  }
  @media (max-width: 1000px) {
    .vision-task {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 1.5rem;
    }
    .task-pair {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    }
    .cue-grid {
      gap: 0.75rem;
    }
    .cue {
      padding: 0.8rem;
    }
    .section-heading {
      gap: 1.5rem;
    }
    .score-grid > div {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 0.6rem;
    }
    .pixel-examples {
      gap: 1rem;
    }
    .pixel-examples > div {
      align-items: flex-start;
      flex-direction: column;
    }
  }
  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .task-pair,
    .section-heading,
    .cue-grid,
    .recipe-grid,
    .correlation-layout,
    .score-grid,
    .chart-grid,
    .result-reading,
    .pixel-examples {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
    .vision-task {
      padding: 1.2rem;
    }
    .task-card {
      padding: 1rem;
    }
    .cue {
      padding: 1rem;
    }
    .cue > img {
      aspect-ratio: 1.5;
    }
    .pixel-examples > div {
      flex-direction: row;
      align-items: center;
    }
    .pixel-examples {
      gap: 1.5rem;
    }
    .score-grid > div {
      grid-template-columns: auto 1fr;
      gap: 1rem;
    }
    .score {
      font-size: 2.7rem;
    }
    .score-grid .eyebrow {
      font-size: 0.55rem;
    }
    .chart-grid figure {
      padding: 0.8rem;
    }
    .image-cues,
    .vision-pipeline,
    .vision-results {
      margin-top: 2.2rem;
    }
    .result-reading {
      gap: 0;
    }
  }
</style>
